{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Hilbert Syzygy Theorem.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Hilbert Syzygy Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"hilbert syzygy theorem hilbert syzygy theorem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Hilbert Syzygy Theorem.md","fileName":"Hilbert Syzygy Theorem.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Hilbert Syzygy Theorem","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 20:15\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Regular Sequences and Syzygies]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Sequences and Syzygies","Syzygy Module","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Hilbert Syzygy Theorem.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Hilbert Syzygy Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"hilbert syzygy theorem hilbert syzygy theorem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Hilbert Syzygy Theorem.md","fileName":"Hilbert Syzygy Theorem.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Hilbert Syzygy Theorem","heading":"Hilbert Syzygy Theorem","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"The graded Hilbert syzygy theorem states that every finitely generated graded module over K[x₁,…,xₙ] has a finite graded free resolution of length at most n.\n\nAny two minimal graded resolutions are isomorphic. Thus polynomial rings impose a uniform bound on how many successive layers of [[Syzygy Module]]s are needed to describe a finitely generated module.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Regular Sequences and Syzygies","Syzygy Module","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Hilbert Syzygy Theorem.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Hilbert Syzygy Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"hilbert syzygy theorem hilbert syzygy theorem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Hilbert Syzygy Theor